# company/ — the per-company prose slots

Every file in this directory is **yours to author**. The kernel stamps a blank
starting point once, when the clone is created, and never touches these files
again: `cs update` adds a slot only if it is missing, so nothing you write here
is overwritten, and you are never asked about it.

They hold the variance `manifest.toml` cannot express. The manifest carries
values — addresses, adapters, knobs. These files carry the sentences somebody
has to write down, and the skills read them at run time to know what is true of
{{ company_name }}.

| File | Read by | Holds |
|---|---|---|
| `triage-domain-examples.md` | `/cs-triage-mail` | what an ordinary mail to {{ email_address }} is about |
| `triage-answerable.md` | `/cs-triage-mail` | which asks may be answered without a human |
| `team-conventions.md` | `/cs-triage-mail` | who replies from where — what the Sent-archive checks cannot see |
| `campaign-product-notes.md` | `/cs-campaign-tick` | your own automatic replies, so they are never read as a human answer |
| `operator-out-of-scope.md` | `/cs-operator` | what the unattended tick must escalate and never do itself |
| `drive-visible-note.md` | `/cs-find-document` | which Shared Drives this project may search |
| `claude-extra.md` | `CLAUDE.md` | other systems this operator may reach, and how |

Rules for all of them:

- **Prose only, and no secrets.** Voice, signature and reply policy are not here
  either: they live in the engine profile's `USER_NOTES`
  (`cs rpc settings.update`), outside every repo.
- **Write what is true of {{ company_name }} and of nobody else.** An agent
  reads these files as fact. Another company's habits pasted in here become
  {{ company_name }}'s habits.
- **A slot left as delivered teaches the agent nothing.** Each file opens with
  "What to write here" and closes with what goes wrong while it is empty. Start
  from those two sections and delete them once the file says something real.
